{"type":"event","event":{"type":"thinking_end","text":"The current `retry.py`:\n- Catches all `Exception` (should only catch `TimeoutError`, `ConnectionError`, `OSError`)\n- Sleeps even after the last attempt (should sleep only between attempts)\n- Returns `None` after exhausting attempts (should re-raise the final transient error)\n- Doesn't raise `ValueError` when `attempts < 1`\n\nNote: `TimeoutError` and `ConnectionError` are both subclasses of `OSError` in Python 3. So catching `OSError` alone would suffice, but for clarity I'll catch all three explicitly (or just `(TimeoutError, ConnectionError, OSError)`).\n\nAlso non-transient errors propagate immediately — since we only catch those three, everything else propagates naturally.\n\nLet me implement:\n\n```python\nimport time\n\ndef retry(fn, attempts=3, base_delay=0.1, sleep=time.sleep):\n    if attempts < 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"attempts must be >= 1\")\n    for i in range(attempts):\n        try:\n            return fn()\n        except (TimeoutError, ConnectionError, OSError):\n            if i == attempts - 1:\n                raise\n            sleep(base_delay * (2 ** i))\n```\n\nWait — \"attempts < 1 raises ValueError\". Should attempts=0 raise ValueError? Yes, per the task.\n\nSleep only between attempts: on the last attempt, if it fails, re-raise without sleeping. That's handled by `if i == attempts - 1: raise`.\n\nLet me verify with the public test:\n- f fails once with TimeoutError (i=0, not last, sleep(None)), then succeeds → returns 7. ✓\n\nLet me write this and test."}}
